.\" La page de manuel de subedit-cli.
.\"
.\" **Courte, et c'est delibere.** Le manuel complet vit sous
.\" share/subedit/manual et fait deux mille lignes ; le recopier ici en
.\" produirait une seconde copie a tenir, qui divergerait. Cette page dit ce
.\" qu'une page de manuel dit : la forme d'appel, ce que chaque sous-commande
.\" fait, les options globales, les codes de retour, puis ou lire le reste.
.\"
.\" Le numero de version est injecte par configure_file depuis le
.\" project(VERSION) de CMakeLists.txt. La liste des sous-commandes, elle, est
.\" confrontee a celle du binaire par check-installation.sh : une sous-commande
.\" ajoutee sans etre ecrite ici fait echouer la porte.
.\"
.\" **Ce fichier s'ecrit sans accents, contrairement au reste du projet.**
.\" troff lit des octets, pas de l'UTF-8, tant que personne n'appelle preconv
.\" pour lui ; il voit alors du latin1, se tait sur ce qui s'y encode, et
.\" avertit sur le reste. Un tiret cadratin dans ce bloc de commentaires l'a
.\" fait avertir depuis le jour ou cette page existe, sans que rien le dise --
.\" issue #268. Le francais accentue passait par chance et non par regle, les
.\" lettres accentuees s'encodant justement dans ce que troff sait lire : c'est
.\" une distinction qu'on ne veut pas avoir a tenir ligne a ligne, donc la page
.\" reste en ASCII de bout en bout, commentaires compris.
.\"
.\" Ce qui la tient : check-installation.sh passe la page installee a
.\" "groff -ww -z" et refuse le moindre avertissement.
.TH SUBEDIT-CLI 1 "" "subedit @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
subedit\-cli \- read, inspect and retime subtitle files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B subedit\-cli
.RI [ global\ options ]
.I subcommand
.RI [ options ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B subedit\-cli
reads SubRip and WebVTT subtitle files, recognising the format from the content
rather than the extension and the encoding from the bytes, and writes them back
out shifted, transformed, re\-timed or cleaned.
.PP
Every subcommand takes several files and treats each one independently: the
failure of one does not stop the others.
.PP
.B Nothing is ever written without an explicit destination.
The six subcommands that write take one of
.BR \-\-output ,
.B \-\-output\-dir
or
.BR \-\-in\-place ,
and refuse to run without one.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B inspect
Report what a subtitle file is made of. Writes nothing.
.TP
.B convert
Write a subtitle file out in another format or shape.
.TP
.B shift
Move every position of a file by a fixed amount.
.TP
.B transform
Correct every position from two points known to be right.
.TP
.B framerate
Re\-time a file mastered at one frame rate for another.
.TP
.B snap
Move every position onto the nearest frame of a frame rate.
.TP
.B hearing-impaired
Remove the sounds described between brackets or parentheses.
.PP
.BR framerate " and " snap
take the same arguments and do opposite things: the first re\-times a file
whose timing is wrong, the second moves each position onto the nearest frame of
a file whose timing is right. Getting one for the other raises no error.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
They are written
.B before
the subcommand, and nowhere else: after it, they are refused as unexpected
arguments.
.B \-\-help
is not an exception to that \(em each subcommand carries one of its own, and
that is the one it prints.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit; the subcommand's own help if one is named.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Say nothing but errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-encoding " NAME"
Read the files in that encoding instead of detecting one. Any name ICU knows is
accepted, aliases included \(em
.BR cp1252 ", " windows\-1252 " and " "WINDOWS 1252"
name one encoding \(em and a name nothing can convert is refused before a file
is read. A byte order mark wins over this option, and the reading says so.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-vv ", " \-vvv
Say more. One is the default; two adds what was recognised of each file; three
adds the bytes read and written, and every diagnostic the reading ran into.
.PP
.B \-\-quiet
together with
.B \-v
is refused: two opposite intentions are not arbitrated in favour of whichever
was written last.
.SH ENCODINGS
A subtitle file declares its encoding nowhere \(em there is no header and no
naming convention \(em except through a byte order mark, which only the Unicode
encodings carry.
.PP
Reading therefore goes in that order: the mark if there is one, then UTF\-8 if
the bytes decode as UTF\-8, then what weighing the bytes proposes. What was
settled is written in the report of
.B inspect
and in the diagnostics of every subcommand, because a detection is a proposal
and never a certainty: Latin\-1 and CP1252 are the same encoding on a text that
does not use the range 0x80\-0x9f.
.PP
.B \-\-encoding
imposes what is read;
.B \-\-to\-encoding
chooses what
.B convert
writes. Without the second, a file is written back in the encoding it was read
in, so that a file rewritten with nothing asked of it gives back the same bytes.
A character the chosen encoding cannot write stops that file, and nothing is
written for it: a
.B ?
put in its place would be text lost in silence.
.SH OUTPUT
The standard output carries the result and nothing else \(em the report of
.BR inspect .
The standard error carries everything else: the narration, the warnings and the
errors. That split is what lets a report be redirected without the account of
what happened going with it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error \(em unknown option, invalid value, forbidden combination. Detected
before any file is touched, so a batch is never left half done.
.TP
.B 2
No file could be treated.
.TP
.B 3
Some files were treated, others were not.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @SUBEDIT_MANUAL_DIR@
The complete manual, in Markdown. It is exhaustive where this page is not:
every option, every accepted value, every message, with a real example per
subcommand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR subedit\-gui (1),
the window this program shares its core with, and the manual named above.
